\ parse-word, parse, and postpone.
\
\ These are the input-stream words: parse-word / parse let Forth code
\ read the source text directly, and postpone defers a word's
\ compilation semantics. Together with the existing `evaluate` they let
\ new notation be defined from inside Forth rather than patched into the
\ engine in C. The driver feeds the whole file to one ff_eval, so a
\ parsing word can read across what look like separate lines.

\ parse-word returns the next whitespace token as text — not a
\ dictionary lookup, unlike `'`.
parse-word hello strlen .          \ 5

\ parse reads up to (and consumes) a delimiter char; 41 is ')'.
41 parse abc) strlen .             \ 3

\ postpone an immediate word: `endif` becomes a working alias for `then`.
: endif postpone then ; immediate
: pick-max { ( a b -- m ) a b > if a else b endif } ;
9 4 pick-max .                     \ 9
3 8 pick-max .                     \ 8

\ postpone a non-immediate word from inside an immediate defining word:
\ each `lay+` lays a `+` into the definition being compiled.
: lay+ postpone + ; immediate
: add3 lay+ lay+ ;                 \ compiles to  ( a b c -- a+b+c )
1 2 3 add3 .                       \ 6

\ Conditional compilation, defined entirely in Forth with parse-word:
\ a false `[if]` skips tokens up to the matching `[then]`.
: [if] ( flag -- )
  0= if
    begin
      parse-word
      dup strlen 0= if drop exit then
      "[then]" strcmp 0=
    until
  then ; immediate
: [then] ; immediate

0 [if]  : skipped 111 ;  [then]
1 [if]  : kept    222 ;  [then]
kept .                             \ 222
